module gunbc.spark.cell_role

import std.types { String, Bool, List, NonEmptyStr }
import product.placement_supply { HostIdentity, host_identity_eq }
import gunbc.fleet_intent_network { operator_host_srv5, operator_host_srv6 }

// SPARK-PAIR-0 P0. The single authority for what each DGX Spark unit is FOR. Desired serving hosts
// and, later, the training offer roster are both projections of this one assignment — neither
// authors a host list of its own. Role is a sum rather than a pair of predicates, so a cell holding
// both roles has no constructor to be written with.

// A CELL HAS ONE ROLE, AND THAT IS A CONSTRUCTION FACT RATHER THAN A CHECKED ONE.
//
// The shape this replaces is the one that was never written down at all: serving desired state named
// both hosts, and the intent to make one of them a trainer lived in nobody's model. The obvious
// repair -- a `serves: Bool` beside a `trains: Bool` -- is the state-space conflation the ledger
// already names. Two booleans admit four states, of which two are wrong: a cell that both serves and
// trains contends for one unified memory pool with no arbiter, and a cell that does neither is a
// machine nothing accounts for. Both would then need a validator, and a validator is what §5 says to
// prefer construction over.
//
// So the roles are a closed sum. There is no `SparkDualRoleCell`, and there is no absent arm: a host
// that is not assigned here is not a Spark cell, which the assignment lookup answers as Absent rather
// than as a third role.
//
// A ROLE IS CONVERGED DESIRED STATE, NOT A DEDICATION.
//
// CORRECTED 2026-09-02 BY OPERATOR DECISION, and the correction is to the FRAME rather than to one
// row. The predecessor note said dynamic role switching was deliberately not modelled and that the
// only transition this project performs is a ONE-WAY retirement of serving from srv6. The operator's
// instruction is the opposite of both halves: "i wouldn't dedicate a whole node for any task - just
// have it converge and serve whatever task we converge it to." Training remains a modelled
// capability -- gunbc.spark.training_ready still carries retirement, its readiness proof and its
// remedies -- but it stops being a NODE RESERVATION, and the reservation was the premature part.
//
// So a row here is desired state that convergence actuates, and editing one is the ordinary way a
// cell changes what it is for. What survives unchanged is everything that made the sum the right
// shape: a cell still holds exactly ONE role at a time, because the contention that motivated it is
// physical and did not go away -- two roles on one unified memory pool have no arbiter. The closed
// sum is what makes that a construction fact rather than a checked one, and there is still no
// `SparkDualRoleCell` and no absent arm.
//
// WHAT IS STILL NOT MODELLED, stated as a gap rather than as scope: a cell IN TRANSITION. Nothing
// here represents mid-flight, so a role edit is a discrete change of desired state and the
// convergence that follows it is what makes it real on the host. A bounded temporary-role
// authority -- a probe that borrows a cell and whose teardown restores the prior role -- is the
// next rung and does not exist yet. Until it does, a serving deployment that wants a training cell
// must change the assignment, not run beside it.

type SparkCellRole
  = SparkServingCell
  | SparkTrainingCell

type SparkCellRoleAssignment {
  host: HostIdentity
  role: SparkCellRole
}

// THE ASSIGNMENT IS AN OPERATOR DECISION, RECORDED WITH ITS BASIS.
//
// srv5 serves and srv6 trains. The two units are physically identical, so nothing measured decides
// which is which -- the same situation as the router-slot allocation in gunbc.spark.dgx_procurement,
// and recorded the same way for the same reason: an arbitrary choice written down as attributable is
// honest, while the same choice presented as derived reads as a fact somebody established.
//
// The basis is that srv5 already carries the live serving occurrence. Assigning serving to the host
// that already serves makes the serving half of this project a model upgrade on a converged host,
// and confines the retirement -- the part with no existing vocabulary -- to one machine.
data spark_cell_role_assignment_basis: NonEmptyStr = "OPERATOR DECISION (SPARK-PAIR-0, 2026-09-02): srv5 and srv6 both serve. This SUPERSEDES the 2026-08-29 decision that assigned srv6 to training; the operator withdrew it as premature -- 'i wouldn't dedicate a whole node for any task - just have it converge and serve whatever task we converge it to' -- and moved training to its own lane. The units are identical, so neither assignment was ever a measurement. Consequence in gunbc.spark.serving_converge_plan: srv6 now receives full serving desired members rather than the baseline-only members a training cell receives." as NonEmptyStr

data srv5_cell_role: SparkCellRoleAssignment = SparkCellRoleAssignment {
  host: operator_host_srv5,
  role: SparkServingCell,
}

data srv6_cell_role: SparkCellRoleAssignment = SparkCellRoleAssignment {
  host: operator_host_srv6,
  role: SparkServingCell,
}

// SPARK-BATCH-3 (operator, 2026-09-05): the goal for every unit is the desired serving state, IN
// PAIRS, serving DeepSeek V4 Flash. None of the five newer units is assigned a cell role yet, and
// that is a refusal, not an omission: srv7/srv8 hold an undischarged llama.cpp RPC peer reservation
// (gunbc.spark.llama_cpp_rpc_observed), which the disjointness wall in
// test.claim.spark_llama_cpp_rpc_witness forbids from entering the serving roster until a release
// receipt exists; and the serving desired state is still per-host Ollama, which is not the pair
// deployment the operator wants. Roles land when pair-serving is promoted to desired and pairing
// is observed from the fabric, so that assigning a role converges a host toward the right thing.
data spark_cell_role_assignments: List<SparkCellRoleAssignment> = [
  srv5_cell_role,
  srv6_cell_role,
]

// THE ONE PLACE THE VARIANTS ARE ENUMERATED IS THE WIRE PROJECTION, AND EQUALITY READS IT.
//
// The shape this replaces was a hand-rolled 4-arm predicate matching the coproduct against itself.
// It is a second enumeration of the same two variants beside this projection, so adding a role would
// require both to be edited and only one of them would fail to compile if the other were forgotten.
// Deriving equality from the canonical projection leaves ONE place that knows what the variants are.
//
// This is the idiom the corpus already uses for exactly this question --
// gunbc.spark.serving_membership's address equality and serving_execution_schedule's fact equality
// are both wire-equality over their own canonical projection -- so it is reuse rather than a local
// convention invented here.
fn spark_cell_role_wire(role: SparkCellRole) -> String {
  match role {
    SparkServingCell => "serving"
    SparkTrainingCell => "training"
  }
}

fn spark_cell_role_eq(a: SparkCellRole, b: SparkCellRole) -> Bool {
  spark_cell_role_wire(role: a) == spark_cell_role_wire(role: b)
}

// ABSENT IS "NOT A SPARK CELL", WHICH IS NOT A ROLE -- AND TWO ROWS FOR ONE HOST IS NEITHER.
//
// Answering a role for an unassigned host would make every non-Spark machine in the fleet a candidate
// for whichever arm the default picked. So the lookup is a sum, and it has three arms rather than an
// optional's two.
//
// The third arm is the one worth explaining. Each ROW carries a sum-valued role, so no single row can
// say a cell both serves and trains -- but the ROSTER is an ordinary list, and two rows naming one
// host can. An earlier fold answered the last match and called it the role: last-row-wins, in a
// lookup the training offer and the production planner both authorize against. That the current
// roster has no duplicate is a fact about today's data, checked by a claim that is outside the
// required gate; it is not a property of the lookup. Ambiguity is therefore an ANSWER here, carrying
// the rows that conflict, and every authorization consumer must match it and refuse.
type SparkCellRoleLookup
  = SparkCellRoleAbsent
  | SparkCellRoleUnique { role: SparkCellRole }
  | SparkCellRoleAmbiguous { assignments: List<SparkCellRoleAssignment> }

// THE LOOKUP IS OVER A ROSTER, AND THE PRODUCTION ROSTER IS ONE APPLICATION OF IT.
//
// Split out 2026-09-02 for a reason the roster change exposed: every claim about role SCOPING read
// the production assignments, so the evidence for the training arm existed only while a production
// host happened to hold that role. The operator withdrawing srv6's dedication would then have
// silently retired a behaviour's only witness -- the arm still exists and still decides desired
// state, and nothing would have exercised it. A capability's evidence must not depend on which
// machines are currently assigned what.
fn spark_cell_role_in(
  assignments: List<SparkCellRoleAssignment>,
  host: HostIdentity,
) -> SparkCellRoleLookup {
  let matched = filter(assignments, a => host_identity_eq(a: a.host, b: host))
  if length(matched) > 1 {
    SparkCellRoleAmbiguous { assignments: matched }
  } else {
    fold(matched, init: SparkCellRoleAbsent, f: fn(acc, a) { SparkCellRoleUnique { role: a.role } })
  }
}

fn spark_cell_role_of(host: HostIdentity) -> SparkCellRoleLookup {
  spark_cell_role_in(assignments: spark_cell_role_assignments, host: host)
}

// THE ROSTER-PARAMETERISED FORM, AND IT IS THE SAME ARGUMENT ONE LEVEL UP.
//
// `spark_cell_role_in` exists because a capability's evidence must not depend on which machines are
// currently assigned what. That argument does not stop at a single host's lookup: every claim about
// a ROLE POPULATION -- how many cells hold a role, whether two role rosters partition a scope --
// had the same dependency, and the operator withdrawing srv6's training dedication took the only
// training cell with it. Four claims in `v2.test.claim.spark_observation_scope` went red on that
// change alone, having asserted `length(training) == 1` against the production roster. The
// behaviour they describe did not change; the machines did.
fn spark_hosts_with_role_in(
  assignments: List<SparkCellRoleAssignment>,
  role: SparkCellRole,
) -> List<HostIdentity> {
  flat_map(assignments, a =>
    match spark_cell_role_in(assignments: assignments, host: a.host) {
      SparkCellRoleUnique { role: r } =>
        if spark_cell_role_eq(a: r, b: role) { [a.host] } else { [] }
      SparkCellRoleAmbiguous { assignments: _ } => []
      SparkCellRoleAbsent => []
    })
}

fn spark_hosts_with_role(role: SparkCellRole) -> List<HostIdentity> {
  spark_hosts_with_role_in(assignments: spark_cell_role_assignments, role: role)
}

fn spark_serving_cell_hosts() -> List<HostIdentity> {
  spark_hosts_with_role(role: SparkServingCell)
}

// THE OFFER ROSTER, AND THERE IS NO PREDICATE BESIDE IT.
//
// A `spark_host_offerable_for_training(host) -> Bool` stood below this, matching the lookup sum into
// true/false. It had exactly one caller: its own claim. The justification offered for keeping it was
// that P3's supply module would consume it -- which is the authored-ahead-of-its-consumer shape
// trimmed from the curl, pgrep and ss rows earlier in this same stack, and it is no better for being
// a function rather than a constant.
//
// This roster is the canonical surface and answers the same question without a second vocabulary: a
// host is offerable exactly when it appears here, and this list already excludes serving cells,
// unassigned hosts, and hosts the roster cannot decide. When P3 needs the offer population it takes
// the list; if it then needs membership of one host, that question gets its answer when a caller
// actually exists to ask it.
fn spark_training_cell_hosts() -> List<HostIdentity> {
  spark_hosts_with_role(role: SparkTrainingCell)
}
